On paper, the survey name, team members, and species list either live in the
collector's memory or get rewritten on every sheet. In NAHPU, you enter this
shared context once before the first specimen, and every record that follows
links back to it.

In this tutorial, you will set up:

- a project for the 2014 Mt. Tujuh survey;
- personnel records for Heru and the field team, with Heru as `Cataloger`;
- a taxon registry with one clean spelling for each name.

## 1. Create the project

{% steps %}

1. On the home screen, select `Create project` > `Create new project`.
2. Fill in the project details:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | Project name | `Mt. Tujuh small mammals 2014` |
    | Description | `Small-mammal survey of Mt. Tujuh, Kerinci, Jambi, 9–18 September 2014. Entered from the paper catalog of Heru Handika.` |
    | Time zone | `Asia/Jakarta` |

    Jambi uses Western Indonesia Time (WIB), which corresponds to
    `Asia/Jakarta`. Setting the time zone documents the clock behind recorded
    times—such as trap-check times on specimen forms—without altering the times
    themselves.

3. Choose `Mammalogy` as the main taxon format so specimen forms display the
   same standard measurements and reproductive fields as the paper catalog.
4. Choose a personnel Field ID. Heru numbered specimens with his initials and a
   running counter, so this mode reproduces `HH 436`, `HH 437`, and so on.
5. Complete the Cataloger details when prompted (see the next section), review
   the summary, and select `Create project`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="tip" %}
If your NAHPU installation already holds active research data, give this
practice project a clear label—such as `Mt. Tujuh tutorial`—so it stays easy to
tell apart.
{% /aside %}

## 2. Add personnel

Heru is the `Cataloger`: he numbered, prepared, and identified the specimens.
His teammates helped set and check trap lines, while the local guide and
boatman made the 16–18 September work at Pasir Putih possible—even though
neither appears anywhere in the paper catalog.

{% steps %}

1. On the project dashboard, select `Add personnel` > `Add new`.
2. Add Heru:

    | Field | Value |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Name*` | `Heru Handika` |
    | Role | `Cataloger` |
    | `Initials` | `HH` |
    | `Cataloger number*` | `436` |

    `Cataloger number` is the next number NAHPU proposes. Setting it to `436`
    makes the first specimen `HH436`, matching the paper catalog, and NAHPU
    increments it by one for each new record. Because these tutorials skip
    numbers, you will override the proposed number in
    [Basic tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/basic/specimens/).

3. Add the remaining team members with role `None` (they did not catalog
   specimens):

    | Name | Role in the survey |
    | --- | --- |
    | `Bima Sampel` | Set and checked lines with Heru |
    | `Citra Contoh` | Set and checked lines |
    | `Dian Teladan` | Checked the Pasir Putih lines |
    | `Eko Pemandu` | Local guide |
    | `Fajar Nelayan` | Fisherman who ran the boat to Pasir Putih |

4. Confirm that all six people appear on the project dashboard.

{% /steps %}

The dashboard now shows the project and its personnel. Heru's cataloger number
reads `530` below because the screenshot was captured after all seven tutorial
specimens were entered:

![NAHPU Project dashboard on an iPad for Mt. Tujuh small mammals 2014, with Heru Handika as Cataloger and the teammates in Personnel](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/project-dashboard.webp)

{% aside type="caution" %}
You cannot change the `Cataloger` role after creating a person. Personnel
records are also shared across every project in your NAHPU installation, so
editing a person's record later updates it everywhere.
{% /aside %}

Event-specific roles—such as leader, helper, local guide, and boat operator—are
assigned per collecting event in [Basic tutorial 4](/en/tutorials/basic/events/).

## 3. Register the taxa

The paper catalog spells *Maxomys hylomyoides* at least five different ways. A
spreadsheet typed straight from those sheets would treat them as five separate
"species". In NAHPU, specimen identifications are chosen from the taxon
registry, enforcing one consistent spelling across the entire project.

Register the corrected names below rather than the paper spellings:

| Paper spelling | Rank | Name to register | Order | Family |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Lariscur insignis`, `Larscur insignis` | Species | *Lariscus insignis* | Rodentia | Sciuridae |
| `Mur crociduroider` | Species | *Mus crociduroides* | Rodentia | Muridae |
| `Maxorys`, `Maxomy`, `Maxotys hylomyoider` | Species | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | Rodentia | Muridae |
| `Maxomys cf. hylomyoider` | Genus | *Maxomys* | Rodentia | Muridae |
| `Crocidura beccari`, `beccarii` | Species | *Crocidura beccarii* | Eulipotyphla | Soricidae |
| `Crocidura neglecta` | Species | *Crocidura neglecta* | Eulipotyphla | Soricidae |

All six belong to the class `Mammalia`.

{% steps %}

1. On the dashboard, open `Taxon registry` and select `Add taxon`.
2. Keep `Manual` selected and choose the `Taxon rank`.
3. Fill in the taxonomic fields from `Class` down to `Specific epithet` (leave
   `Specific epithet` empty for the genus-level *Maxomys* entry).
4. Optionally add a common name, such as `Three-striped ground squirrel` for
   *Lariscus insignis*.
5. Repeat for each row, then verify that the registry lists six entries.

{% /steps %}

The genus-level *Maxomys* entry is for HH 523, which Heru labeled
`cf. hylomyoides`—meaning it resembled *M. hylomyoides*, but he was unsure of
the species. Recording the genus keeps the identification honest to the
evidence; in [Basic tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/basic/specimens/), you will put
the `cf.` qualifier in the specimen's remarks.

{% aside type="tip" %}
For longer species lists, prepare a CSV or Excel file and use
`Add taxon` > `Import`. See
[Import a taxon list](/en/how-to-recipes/prepare/import-taxa/).
{% /aside %}

## What you gained over paper

- The survey has a clear name, description, and declared time zone, so future
  readers know what every recorded time means.
- Everyone who contributed to the survey—including the local guide and
  boatman—is on record.
- Every taxon has a single authoritative spelling; fixing a typo in the registry
  updates every specimen linked to it.

